&lt;li&gt;Partner with Product and GTM to understand enterprise security requirements and translate them into product investments that unlock adoption without compromising Warp&#39;s usability.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set technical direction for enterprise security at Warp, identifying the highest-leverage capabilities and architectural investments as our product and enterprise business grow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bring security expertise to the broader engineering organization, helping teams make good architectural decisions around authentication, authorization, isolation, data protection, and other security-sensitive systems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Must Have&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;5+ years of professional software engineering experience, ideally building complex production systems in a fast-paced product or startup environment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Strong software engineering fundamentals and a track record of personally designing, building, and shipping significant product or platform capabilities.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience building security-sensitive enterprise features or systems, such as authentication and authorization, RBAC/ABAC, SSO/SCIM, permissions and policy systems, audit logging, tenant isolation, secrets management, encryption, or data controls.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Strong security intuition and the ability to reason deeply about trust boundaries, permissions, credentials, data access, and how systems can be misused.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Strong product judgment: you can understand what enterprise customers are trying to accomplish and translate security requirements into pragmatic, usable product experiences.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ability to lead ambiguous technical areas as a senior IC: identify the problem, develop an approach, align stakeholders, and drive implementation through completion.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clear, thoughtful communication across engineering, Product, GTM, and technically sophisticated customers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Comfortable operating with significant ownership and ambiguity—you default to figuring things out rather than waiting to be told what to do.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
